{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The Oregon Short Line, Union Pacific's original gateway from its transcontinental main line (at Granger, WY) across southern Idaho to the Pacific Northwest (Huntington, OR), completed 1884 -- a year before the Northern Pacific's own route through the Idaho panhandle finished (see northern_pacific_idaho_panhandle.geojson) and roughly matching Idaho's 1900-era rail map. Idaho's capital, Boise, was notably NOT on the through mainline at first: the mainline ran further south via Orchard, and Boise was reached only by the Nampa-Boise branch (opened 1887 as the independent Idaho Central Railway) until the 1924-25 'Boise Cutoff' extended that branch east to rejoin the mainline at Orchard, finally routing through trains into the city. The mainline feature's geometry below still traces the original 1884 bypass-Boise alignment throughout, since that corridor remains active rail today; the 1925-on segments' notes flag that actual passenger routing shifted onto the Boise Branch geometry instead. Geometry traced through real track using OpenStreetMap data (Overpass API) and shortest-path graph search chaining real way segments -- both lines are still active UP freight routes. Traced main line length (864.9 km / 537.4 mi) closely matches the historically cited ~540-mile Oregon Short Line, corroborating the routing.", "source": "Wikipedia (Oregon Short Line Railroad, Portland Rose (train), Pioneer (train), North Coast Limited), utahrails.net (OSL corporate history, UP passenger timeline), spokesman.com ('The Pioneer Ends Its 114-Year Run', May 12 1997), idaho.gov (Idaho Central Railroad reference series), railfan.com (Boise Depot Celebrates 100 Years / Boise Cutoff history)", "pulled": "20260818", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Oregon Short Line Railroad", "line": "Oregon Short Line: Granger, WY \u2013 Huntington, OR", "id": "osl-granger-huntington", "color": "#F2A900", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 55, "speed_source": "estimated: secondary transcontinental-gateway main line through mountainous terrain, slower than a premium flagship route; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1884, "end": 1930, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Opened 1884, connecting the Union Pacific main line at Granger, WY across southern Idaho to Huntington, OR; general/named service including the Portland Limited; until the 1925 Boise Cutoff, this route bypassed Boise via Orchard -- see the companion Nampa-Boise Branch feature for how the capital was served; frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Oregon Short Line Railroad, Portland Rose (train), Pioneer (train), North Coast Limited), utahrails.net (OSL \u2026"]}, {"start": 1930, "end": 1935, "headway_min": 720, "note": "The Portland Limited was renamed the Portland Rose in 1930; by this point mainline passenger trains had shifted onto the post-1925 Boise routing (not reflected in this feature's own geometry, traced along the original 1884 alignment); frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Oregon Short Line Railroad, Portland Rose (train), Pioneer (train), North Coast Limited), utahrails.net (OSL \u2026"]}, {"start": 1935, "end": 1971, "headway_min": 360, "note": "Streamliner peak: Union Pacific's City of Portland (1935) joined the Portland Rose on this corridor; all Union Pacific passenger service here was discontinued Apr 30 1971, the day before Amtrak began operations; frequency estimated, unsourced", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Oregon Short Line Railroad, Portland Rose (train), Pioneer (train), North Coast Limited), utahrails.net (OSL \u2026"]}, {"start": 1977, "end": 1997, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Amtrak's Pioneer (Seattle/Portland - Boise - Salt Lake City - Denver - Chicago) revived service after a 1971-77 gap with no passenger trains at all; discontinued May 10 1997 after 114 years of continuous Pocatello-Oregon service and never replaced -- Idaho has had no intercity passenger rail since", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "Wikipedia (Oregon Short Line Railroad, Portland Rose (train), Pioneer (train), North Coast Limited), utahrails.net (OSL \u2026"]}], "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-116.5199297, 43.5532845], "to": [-116.7173582, 43.6876013], "chord_mi": 13.66, "traced_mi": 13.55, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["UP Huntington Subdivision (11.25 mi)", "UP Nampa Subdivision (2.3 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"